new file mode 100644
index 0000000..10d8649
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/deployment.md
@@ -0,0 +1,74 @@
+## Deployment
+
+Fizzy deploys to a single Linux server with [Kamal](https://kamal-deploy.org). Everything runs in one container: Puma serves the web requests and runs the Solid Queue background jobs, and the data lives in SQLite databases on a Docker volume. In front of it, kamal-proxy terminates SSL with a certificate from Let's Encrypt.
+
+That setup is described in `config/deploy.yml`. 37signals' own multi-server setup lives in the [SaaS gem](../saas/README.md).
+
+### What you need
+
+- A Linux server you can SSH into as root, with ports 80 and 443 open. `kamal setup` installs Docker on it if it's missing.
+- A hostname with a DNS A record pointing at the server, so Let's Encrypt can issue the certificate.
+- An SMTP server or provider. Fizzy signs people in with a code sent by email, so without it nobody gets in.
+- Docker running on your own machine, to build the image.
+
+### Configuring
+
+Fill in `config/deploy.yml`:
+
+1. Your server's IP address or hostname under `servers`.
+2. Your hostname, as `proxy.host` and again as `APP_HOST`.
+3. Your SMTP server, port and username, and the address email is sent from.
+
+Then provide the secrets. `.kamal/secrets` is committed to the repository, so it doesn't contain any values; it reads them from your environment:
+
+```sh
+export SECRET_KEY_BASE=$(bin/rails secret) # keep it somewhere safe, changing it signs everyone out
+export SMTP_PASSWORD=...
+```
+
+Web push notifications also need a VAPID key pair as `VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY` and `VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY`; see the [development guide](development.md#web-push-notifications) for generating one. Without it, Fizzy runs fine, just without push notifications.
+
+To read the secrets from a password manager instead, see the comments in `.kamal/secrets` and [Kamal's docs](https://kamal-deploy.org/docs/commands/secrets/).
+
+### Deploying
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal setup
+```
+
+This builds the image, prepares the server, boots kamal-proxy and starts Fizzy. The first start creates the databases, and every start after that runs pending migrations.
+
+Open https://your-host and sign up. The first signup creates the account; after that, signups close and people join by invitation.
+
+Later deploys are just:
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal deploy
+```
+
+### Your data
+
+The SQLite databases and uploaded files live in `/rails/storage` in the container, which `config/deploy.yml` mounts on a Docker volume named `fizzy_storage`. Containers are replaced on every deploy; the volume stays, and with it your data.
+
+That volume is what to back up. The image includes the `sqlite3` CLI, so a consistent copy of the primary database, ready to be copied off the server, is one command away:
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal app exec --reuse "sqlite3 storage/production.sqlite3 '.backup storage/production.backup.sqlite3'"
+```
+
+### Useful commands
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal logs     # Tail the logs
+bin/kamal console  # Rails console
+bin/kamal shell    # Shell inside the container
+bin/kamal dbc      # sqlite3 on the primary database
+bin/kamal details  # See what's running
+```
+
+### Variations
+
+- **ARM server**: set `builder.arch` to `arm64`.
+- **Hosted registry**: by default Kamal runs a registry on your machine and tunnels it to the server, so no registry account is needed. `config/deploy.yml` shows how to push to a hosted registry such as ghcr.io instead.
+- **Tuning**: Puma workers and Solid Queue worker processes default to one per CPU core. Override with `WEB_CONCURRENCY` and `JOB_CONCURRENCY` under `env.clear`.
+- **SMTPS**: for a mail server that speaks TLS from the start (usually port 465) rather than STARTTLS, add `SMTP_TLS: true` under `env.clear`.
